> >it is not (n*lg n), it is (d * lg n), where d is the logarithm of n  
> >or larger.
> 
> That is too fast.
> 
> If b is the number of bits/number and there are n numbers, then its  
> complexity is O(bn): linear in the size of the input, with respect to  
> the random-access machine model.

But there is a limit on the size of n. It can only sort 2^^b different numbers.
While it is linear for most applications (where b is a known number) strictly
speaking it is logarithmic, as the number of possible different numbers to be
sorted is 2^^b. 
	It is logarithmic to comp sci purists (which sometimes includes me).
Practically, it is linear, but irritating to implement, and not necessarily 
worth the trouble.
