> Translating from GS-talk into everyday language, doesn't this just mean
> that empirical and logical truths are differ? ie the difference
> between "2 + 2 = 4" and "cold water freezes"?  I don't need GS to tell
> me that.
 
Maybe you don't.  A lot of people do.

> Sorry Eric, all this GS business sounds similar to Objectivism in the
> sense that you've invented new terms for old concepts and think you've
> uncovered new truths.

I've never claimed that GS's value lies in "new truths"; it does not.  From
a formal philosophical point of view, it's basically just a knock-off
of the Wittgenstein/Moore style of empiricism plus language analysis.

What makes GS valuable is that it's an accessible, teachable system for
training your brain to *use* the consequences of Wittgenstein/Moore as
a normal, constant part of your mental life.  By doing this, it gives you
excellent bullshit filters, protects you from certain kinds of dangerous
manipulation, and effectively makes you more intelligent.
